    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense, but I'm having trouble visualizing the actual way the air is moving. Can you give me a more detailed breakdown of the air interaction? Like, what is physically happening with the particles/molecules as they hit the leading edge and move around the wing? I want to really get the mechanics of it down.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "As air molecules strike the leading edge, they are split and flow over and under the wing's curved surface. The molecules traveling over the top must accelerate to cover the greater distance in the same amount of time, which is the core of Bernoulli's effect. This increased velocity decreases the kinetic energy density in that region, causing the pressure to drop significantly compared to the slower-moving, higher-pressure air underneath. This pressure differential is the physical mechanism creating the upward force, or lift.",
      "source": "target",
