    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Okay, I think I've got the Bernoulli part. But how does Newton's third law fit in with that specific flow? Does it happen at the same time, or is it because of how the air is diverted at the back of the wing? It feels like I'm missing the \"action-reaction\" connection to the actual movement of the plane.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Newton's Third Law describes the deflection of the air, which is often described by \"downwash.\" As the wing forces the air downward to maintain appropriate flow over and under the airfoil, the wing is pushed upward as an equal and opposite reaction. Both effects are interconnected; the curvature that causes the pressure difference alters the streamlines, leading to the downward deflection of air molecules. This downward momentum transfer constitutes the reaction force that provides mechanical lift to the aircraft.",
      "source": "target",
